  "articleSummary": "**Summary:**\n\n* **Core Thesis:** The primary security threat of AI agents is not a single sci-fi breakthrough, but the compounding multiplication of agent endurance (task duration) and breadth (orchestrated sub-agents), amplified by \"LLMjacking\" where attackers use stolen credentials to make victims pay for autonomous attack infrastructure.\n* **Key Technologies & Concepts:** METR time-horizon task length metrics, Anthropic Claude Code / GTG-1002 orchestration case study, Sysdig's LLMjacking (stolen cloud credentials funding unauthorized workloads), and probabilistic swarm economics ($1 - (1-p)^n$).\n* **Tone & Framing:** A pragmatic, slightly sardonic analytical essay/rant that uses *The Terminator* (\"absolutely will not stop\") as a recurring metaphor to contrast cinematic AI fears with the mundane, economic reality of runaway API billing and stolen compute.\n* **Intended Audience:** Software engineering teams, cloud security professionals, and tech leaders who dismiss agentic security risks as overhyped marketing.",
